### Why is defence in depth necessary?

Each control covers a gap another leaves open. An attacker must defeat all of them at once.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class BruteForceDefenceInDepth {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Each layer catches a different attack:");
        System.out.println("  Rate limit: caps velocity of attempts");
        System.out.println("  Lockout: stops single-account guessing");
        System.out.println("  CAPTCHA: blocks automated scripts");
        System.out.println("  MFA: makes password alone useless");
        System.out.println("  IP blacklist: blocks persistent source");
        System.out.println("  Monitoring: catches credential stuffing across many accounts");
        System.out.println("  Short tokens: limits damage if credentials compromised");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Removing any one layer opens a specific hole.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Each layer catches a different attack:
  Rate limit: caps velocity of attempts
  Lockout: stops single-account guessing
  CAPTCHA: blocks automated scripts
  MFA: makes password alone useless
  IP blacklist: blocks persistent source
  Monitoring: catches credential stuffing across many accounts
  Short tokens: limits damage if credentials compromised

Removing any one layer opens a specific hole.
```

### How does rate limiting work?

Cap the number of login attempts per time window per account and per IP, enforced at the API gateway with Redis.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.time.Duration;

@Service
public class LoginRateLimiter {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 5;
    private static final Duration WINDOW = Duration.ofMinutes(1);

    public LoginRateLimiter(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public boolean isAllowed(String accountId, String ipAddress) {
        String accountKey = "login:account:" + accountId;
        String ipKey = "login:ip:" + ipAddress;

        Long accountAttempts = redis.opsForValue().increment(accountKey);
        Long ipAttempts = redis.opsForValue().increment(ipKey);

        if (accountAttempts == 1) redis.expire(accountKey, WINDOW);
        if (ipAttempts == 1) redis.expire(ipKey, WINDOW);

        boolean allowed = accountAttempts <= MAX_ATTEMPTS && ipAttempts <= 20;
        if (!allowed) {
            System.out.println("Rate limited: account=" + accountAttempts
                    + ", ip=" + ipAttempts);
        }
        return allowed;
    }
}
```

```text
Rate limit configuration:
  Per account: 5 attempts per minute
  Per IP: 20 attempts per minute (shared IPs like corporate NAT)
  Response: 429 Too Many Requests
```

### How does account lockout work without enabling DoS?

Naive lockout lets an attacker intentionally fail logins to lock out a legitimate user. Use progressive delays instead of hard permanent locks.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class ProgressiveLockout {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Progressive lockout (not hard lock):");
        System.out.println("  Failure 1-3: immediate retry allowed");
        System.out.println("  Failure 4: 30 second delay before next attempt");
        System.out.println("  Failure 5: 5 minute delay");
        System.out.println("  Failure 6+: 15 minute delay + notify account owner");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: Permanent lock after 3 failures");
        System.out.println("  Attacker locks out legitimate users intentionally (DoS)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Progressive delay + account-owner notification");
        System.out.println("  Attacker is slowed; real user is notified and can reset");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Progressive lockout (not hard lock):
  Failure 1-3: immediate retry allowed
  Failure 4: 30 second delay before next attempt
  Failure 5: 5 minute delay
  Failure 6+: 15 minute delay + notify account owner
```

### When do you show CAPTCHA?

After a few failed attempts, not on every login. This avoids annoying legitimate users while blocking bots.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class CaptchaStrategy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CAPTCHA trigger strategy:");
        System.out.println("  Attempt 1-2: no CAPTCHA (good UX for legitimate users)");
        System.out.println("  Attempt 3+: show CAPTCHA (blocks automated scripts)");
        System.out.println("  Successful login: reset CAPTCHA counter");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Limitation: CAPTCHA solving services exist");
        System.out.println("  -> CAPTCHA is one layer, not the whole defense");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
CAPTCHA trigger strategy:
  Attempt 1-2: no CAPTCHA (good UX for legitimate users)
  Attempt 3+: show CAPTCHA (blocks automated scripts)
  Successful login: reset CAPTCHA counter
```

### Why is MFA the strongest single control?

Even if the attacker guesses the password, they need a second factor. For high-risk actions, require step-up MFA.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class MfaStrength {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("MFA breaks the economics of brute force:");
        System.out.println("  Without MFA: guess password -> full access");
        System.out.println("  With MFA: guess password -> still blocked by OTP/hardware key");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step-up MFA for high-risk actions:");
        System.out.println("  Login: password + OTP");
        System.out.println("  View balance: JWT only (already authenticated)");
        System.out.println("  Transfer > 100000: re-verify with OTP (step-up)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
MFA breaks the economics of brute force:
  Without MFA: guess password -> full access
  With MFA: guess password -> still blocked by OTP/hardware key

Step-up MFA for high-risk actions:
  Login: password + OTP
  View balance: JWT only (already authenticated)
  Transfer > 100000: re-verify with OTP (step-up)
```

### How does monitoring detect attacks?

Track failed-login rate and lockout counts. Alert when they spike. Detect credential stuffing that per-account lockout misses.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class AttackMonitoring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Metrics to monitor:");
        System.out.println("  login_failure_total (counter, per service)");
        System.out.println("  login_lockout_total (counter, per service)");
        System.out.println("  login_captcha_triggered_total (counter)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Alert rules:");
        System.out.println("  login_failure_total > 1000/min -> credential stuffing attack");
        System.out.println("  login_lockout_total > 100/min -> brute force on many accounts");
        System.out.println("  Single IP with > 50 failures -> auto-blacklist IP");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Metrics to monitor:
  login_failure_total (counter, per service)
  login_lockout_total (counter, per service)
  login_captcha_triggered_total (counter)

Alert rules:
  login_failure_total > 1000/min -> credential stuffing attack
  login_lockout_total > 100/min -> brute force on many accounts
  Single IP with > 50 failures -> auto-blacklist IP
```

### How do short-lived tokens limit damage?

Access tokens expire in minutes. A stolen credential has a tiny window of usefulness.

```java
package com.bank.security;

public class ShortLivedTokens {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Token strategy:");
        System.out.println("  Access token: 5 minutes (short blast radius)");
        System.out.println("  Refresh token: 24 hours (rotated on each use)");
        System.out.println("  Logout: revokes refresh token -> no new access tokens");
        System.out.println("  Current access token expires in <= 5 minutes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Refresh token rotation:");
        System.out.println("  Each use issues new refresh + access tokens");
        System.out.println("  Reuse of old refresh token -> detect theft -> revoke all");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Token strategy:
  Access token: 5 minutes (short blast radius)
  Refresh token: 24 hours (rotated on each use)
  Logout: revokes refresh token -> no new access tokens
  Current access token expires in <= 5 minutes
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Hash passwords with BCrypt or Argon2, return generic failure messages, apply throttling to password-reset and OTP endpoints, and combine signals into a risk score.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] BCrypt or Argon2 for password hashing (slow by design)
  [x] Generic failure: 'Invalid credentials' (never reveal which was wrong)
  [x] Throttle password-reset and OTP endpoints (common blind spots)
  [x] Combine signals: unusual geo + new device + rising failures = step-up MFA
  [x] Progressive delays, not hard permanent locks
  [x] Apply same limits to API keys and service accounts
```
